{
  "id": "SEC-05-source-tax-liability",
  "order": 5,
  "title": "Who is subject to source tax",
  "purpose": "Ask Swiss residents only for the facts that decide liability (nationality, C permit, spouse exemption). Determine for every employee whether they are subject to source tax and which residence the calculator uses.",
  "stories": [
    {
      "id": "US-05.1",
      "title": "Ask nationality and C permit only of employees living in Switzerland",
      "as_a": "hr",
      "i_want": "Lonio to ask an employee who lives in Switzerland for their nationality and, if they are not Swiss, only whether they hold a C permit, and to ask neither of an employee living abroad",
      "so_that": "I know whether a Swiss resident can be subject to source tax without collecting nationality or permit data that no party needs",
      "priority": "P1",
      "priority_reason": "HR today never knows up front who has to answer the source tax questions; without these answers liability cannot be decided and the tariff code cannot be computed (F-hr_transcript-b84498, D-001).",
      "acceptance_criteria": [
        {
          "id": "AC-05.1.1",
          "title": "Nationality is asked only of employees living in Switzerland",
          "given": "an employee has given the country of their home address that applies on the first working day",
          "when": "the country is Switzerland",
          "then": "Lonio asks for the employee's nationality and an answer is required. Anyone who holds Swiss citizenship answers Switzerland, even if they also hold another nationality (decision D-008, an assumption to be verified by a domain expert). When the country is any other country, nationality is not asked, because an employee living abroad is subject to source tax whatever their nationality.",
